.\" raz-tutorial(7) - a tour of raz
.\" Every whole module in a literal block here is a program that make
.\" check compiles and runs, against test/man/Name.expect; see
.\" tools/Doc.raz.
.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt RAZ-TUTORIAL 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm raz-tutorial
.Nd a tour of the raz language
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This tour is for someone who has programmed in Pascal or C and has
never seen raz.
Each section is one idea, with a program to run.
The programs are whole: save one under its module's name and run it.
.Pp
The language is defined by the report,
.Pa doc/raz.md
in raz's sources; where this page is short, the report says it all.
.Xr raz 7
is the language at a glance,
.Xr raz-lib 3
the library, and
.Xr raz 1
the compiler.
.Sh 1. HELLO, WORLD
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Hello;
import Out;

proc main*;
begin
    Out.str("hello, world\en");
end;

end Hello.
.Ed
.Pp
Saved as
.Pa Hello.raz ,
it is compiled and run with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raz Hello.raz && ./Hello
raz run Hello.raz
.Ed
.Pp
the second without writing a file.
A module lives in a file of its own name, and its name comes again
after its last
.Cm end .
.Cm import Out
lets it use what the module
.Cm Out
exports, as
.Cm Out.str .
The
.Cm *
after
.Cm main
exports it; the program starts at the
.Cm main
of the module named on the command line.
Every statement ends in
.Cm \&; ,
even one that ends in
.Cm end .
.Sh 2. MODULES
A program is a set of modules.
Here is one, saved as
.Pa Greet.raz :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Greet;
import Out;

var count*: INT;    (* the greetings so far *)

proc hello*(name: []const U8);
begin
    Out.str("hello, ");
    Out.str(name);
    Out.ln;
    INC(count);
end;

begin
    Out.str("Greet is ready\en");
end Greet.
.Ed
.Pp
and a program beside it that uses it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Twice;
import Out, G := Greet;

proc main*;
begin
    G.hello("world");
    G.hello("raz");
    Out.int(G.count);
    Out.str(" greetings\en");
end;

end Twice.
.Ed
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Greet is ready
hello, world
hello, raz
2 greetings
.Ed
.Pp
A name with
.Cm *
is exported; any other is the module's own.
.Cm "G := Greet"
imports
.Cm Greet
under a shorter name.
Another module may read
.Cm G.count ,
but only
.Cm Greet
may change it.
The statements after a module's last
.Cm begin
are its body, which runs once, before
.Cm main ,
after the bodies of the modules it imports.
.Pp
The compiler finds
.Cm Greet
beside the main module, then in each directory given with
.Fl I ,
then in the library.
The library's module names, such as
.Cm Out , Str
and
.Cm Map ,
are kept for it: a program doesn't name a module of its own so.
.Sh 3. VALUES, TYPES AND CONVERSIONS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Values;
import Fmt;

const Max = 100;    (* untyped: it takes the type it meets *)

proc main*;
var n: INT; b: U8; big: I64; x: F64; c: I32; ok: BOOL;
begin
    n := Max * 3;
    b := 250;
    b := b + 10;          (* wraps, in a U8: 4 *)
    big := n;             (* an INT goes into an I64 *)
    x := F64(n) / 7.0;    (* nothing else converts unasked *)
    c := '\eu{E9}';        (* a character is its code point *)
    ok := (n > Max) and (b # 0);
    Fmt.print("{} {} {} {} {} {}\en", [Fmt.int(n), Fmt.int(I64(b)),
        Fmt.int(big), Fmt.real(x, 3), Fmt.int(I64(c)),
        Fmt.bool(ok)]);
end;

end Values.
.Ed
.Bd -literal -offset indent
300 4 300 42.857 233 true
.Ed
.Pp
The integers are
.Cm I8 , I16 , I32 , I64
and
.Cm U8 , U16 , U32 , U64 ,
and
.Cm INT
and
.Cm UINT ,
as big as an address.
.Cm INT
is the everyday one: lengths, indexes and counts are
.Cm INT Ns s .
The reals are
.Cm F32
and
.Cm F64 .
Arithmetic wraps at the type's width, and never stops the program,
except dividing by zero.
.Pp
Two types never mix but where nothing can be lost:
.Cm INT
goes into
.Cm I64 ,
wherever the one meets the other, so with
.Cm "big: I64" ,
.Cm "big + n"
is an
.Cm I64 ;
and
.Cm U8 , I8
and
.Cm I16 ,
which an
.Cm INT
holds on any machine, go into
.Cm INT ,
so a digit's value is
.Cm "n := n * 10 + s[i] - '0'" .
What an operator made in a small type doesn't, since it may have
wrapped there: with
.Cm "a, b: U8" ,
.Cm "n := a + b"
is an error, and
.Cm "INT(a) + b"
the sum.
With
.Cm "c: I32" ,
.Cm "c < n"
is an error.
A type's name converts, as
.Cm F64(n)
and
.Cm I64(b)
do above; an
.Cm I64
becomes an
.Cm INT
only so, as
.Cm INT(big) ,
since an
.Cm INT
may be smaller on another machine.
A number written in the program is untyped until it meets a type;
.Cm 0FFH
is hexadecimal, and
.Cm 1.5
a real, which an integer never becomes without a conversion.
.Pp
.Cm # ,
not
.Cm <> ,
is
.Dq different .
.Cm and , or
and
.Cm not
are for
.Cm BOOL Ns s ,
and bind below the relations, so
.Cm "i < n and a[i] # 0"
needs no parentheses;
.Cm & , | , xor
and
.Cm ~
are for the bits of integers.
Every variable starts at zero.
.Sh 4. PROCEDURES AND SEVERAL RESULTS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Procs;
import Out;

proc divmod(a, b: INT): (INT, INT);
begin
    return a div b, a mod b;
end;

proc swap(var a, b: INT);
var t: INT;
begin
    t := a;
    a := b;
    b := t;
end;

proc square(n: INT): INT;
begin
    return n * n;
end;

proc twice(f: proc(n: INT): INT; n: INT): INT;
begin
    return f(f(n));
end;

proc main*;
var q, r, x, y: INT;
begin
    q, r := divmod(17, 5);
    x := 1;
    y := 2;
    swap(x, y);
    Out.int(q); Out.char(' '); Out.int(r); Out.ln;
    Out.int(x); Out.char(' '); Out.int(y); Out.ln;
    Out.int(twice(square, 3)); Out.ln;
    _, r := divmod(-7, 2);
    Out.int(r); Out.ln;
end;

end Procs.
.Ed
.Bd -literal -offset indent
3 2
2 1
81
-1
.Ed
.Pp
A parameter is a copy, unless it is
.Cm var ,
when it is the caller's variable itself.
A procedure may return several results, which a multiple assignment
takes apart; the blank,
.Cm _ ,
drops one.
A procedure is a value too, of a procedure type, as
.Cm f
is in
.Cm twice .
A procedure without parameters leaves out the parentheses, as
.Cm Out.ln
does.
Procedures don't nest, and a name is declared before it is used.
.Pp
A call that drops a result, as
.Cm "Sys.write(fd, s);"
does, is warned of;
.Cm "_ := Sys.write(fd, s);"
says it is meant.
.Pp
A record or an array that a procedure only reads goes as
.Cm const :
the caller's variable itself, read in place with nothing copied, which
the procedure can't change.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Grids;
import Out;

type Grid = record cells: [64]INT; w, h: INT; end;

proc fill(var g: Grid; v: INT);    (* changes g *)
var i: INT;
begin
    for i := 0 to g.w * g.h - 1 do g.cells[i] := v; end;
end;

proc sum(const g: Grid): INT;      (* reads g in place *)
var i, s: INT;
begin
    s := 0;
    for i := 0 to g.w * g.h - 1 do s := s + g.cells[i]; end;
    return s;
end;

proc main*;
var g: Grid;
begin
    g.w := 4;
    g.h := 2;
    fill(g, 3);
    Out.int(sum(g)); Out.ln;
end;

end Grids.
.Ed
.Bd -literal -offset indent
24
.Ed
.Pp
So a heading says what a procedure does with what it is given: a value
for a single value or a slice it reads,
.Cm const
for a record or an array it reads,
.Cm var
for what it changes, and a pointer only for what it keeps past the
call.
.Sh 5. ARRAYS, SLICES AND STRINGS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
module Slices;
import Out, Str;

proc sum(s: []INT): INT;
var i, t: INT;
begin
    t := 0;
    for i := 0 to LEN(s) - 1 do t := t + s[i]; end;
    return t;
end;

proc main*;
var a: [5]INT; i: INT; s, val: []const U8; name: [8]U8; ok: BOOL;
begin
    for i := 0 to 4 do a[i] := i * i; end;
    Out.int(sum(a)); Out.ln;        (* the array, as a slice *)
    Out.int(sum(a[1..3])); Out.ln;  (* a[1] and a[2] *)
    s := "hello, world";
    Out.str(s[7..LEN(s)]); Out.ln;
    if s[0..5] = "hello" then Out.str("it says hello\en"); end;
    Out.int(Str.find(s, "world")); Out.ln;
    _, val, ok := Str.cut(" size = 42 ", "=");
    if ok then Out.str(Str.trim(val)); Out.ln; end;
    name := "raz";                  (* a copy, the program's own *)
    name[0] := 'R';
    Out.str(name[0..3]); Out.ln;
    Out.str(\(ga
        C:\etmp\e "as it stands"
          over lines
        \(ga);
end;

end Slices.
.Ed
.Bd -literal -offset indent
